  "faq": [
    {
      "q": "How does a hair dryer make hot air?",
      "a": "A fan blows air across a coil of nichrome wire. The wire heats up as current flows through it, and the passing air carries that heat out of the nozzle.",
      "asks": [
        "What is inside a hair dryer?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "What does the fan in a hair dryer do?",
      "a": "The fan pulls air in at the back and pushes it over the heating coil and out of the front. Moving air dries hair by carrying away the evaporated water.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does a hair dryer need a fan?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does my hair dryer switch off when it gets too hot?",
      "a": "A thermal cut-out senses when the coil is too hot, often because the back grille is blocked by fluff, and cuts the power until it cools.",
      "asks": [
        "Why did my hair dryer stop working suddenly?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "Why does a hair dryer use so much power?",
      "a": "Heating air quickly takes a lot of energy. Most hair dryers draw about 1,800 to 2,400 watts, nearly all of it to heat the coil.",
      "asks": [
        "How many watts is a hair dryer?"
      ]
    },
    {
      "q": "How does hot air dry hair faster?",
      "a": "Warm air can hold more water vapour, and heat gives water molecules the energy to evaporate. Moving air carries the damp air away so more can evaporate.",
      "asks": [
        "Why does warm air dry things?"
      ]
    }
  ],
  "facts": [
    "Typical hair dryers are rated at about 1,800 to 2,400 watts.",
    "A thermal cut-out stops a hair dryer overheating when its air intake is blocked."
  ],
